  "response": "# Checkout rollout plan\n\nI worked only from the scan output and didn't open the source. Where something can't be settled from the scan, I say so.\n\n## 1. Fix first: two different `Basket` types (crime_00001, high, 0.87)\n\nThe checkout half and the core half each have their own `Basket`, and they don't match:\n\n| | `src/checkout/basket.ts:13` | `src/core/types.ts:4` |\n|---|---|---|\n| fields | 5 | 2 |\n| `currency` | `string` | **missing** |\n| `discountCents` | `number` | **missing** |\n\nBoth are exported, both have two importers, and nothing in the build compares them. Both missing fields are money fields. So a basket that leaves checkout and passes through anything typed against core's `Basket` loses its currency and discount, and the compiler won't object. Core may then price it at full price in a default currency.\n\n**What to do:**\n- Pick one shape as the real one. Checkout's 5-field version is probably the true record, so core should either import it or use a named subset like `Pick<…>`. Don't keep two separate declarations.\n- Decide in this same change whether core's code actually needs `currency` and `discountCents`. If anything in core computes totals, it does.\n- `src/core/types.ts:15` has `baskets: Basket` (crime_00018). Check which `Basket` that is and whether it should be an array. You'll be editing that file anyway.\n- Add a test that pins the money fields. The existing test `\"computes a subtotal\"` (`test/session.test.ts:15-19`, crime_00003) has **no assertions at all**. Make it assert a subtotal that includes a discount and a currency. Then it actually guards the fix.\n\n## 2. Fix second: discarded error in `createSession` (crime_00017)\n\nAt `src/checkout/session.ts:80-82`, the POST to `endpoint(\"/sessions\")` sits inside a `try/catch` whose catch block contains only a comment. It doesn't even capture the error in a variable. If session creation fails, the caller sees what looks like success and nothing is logged. A customer could reach the next step with no server-side session. This is the newest code in the repo and it's on the checkout path itself.\n\n**What to do:**\n- Either rethrow, or return a typed failure result that the caller has to handle. Log the error along with the endpoint at a minimum.\n- Also check that `res.ok` is examined. A failure response (like a 4xx/5xx) doesn't throw in `fetch`, so it gets past the catch too.\n- Add a test for the failure path. The scan scores `test_gap` as 0 here, but don't trust that: all three tests in `session.test.ts` are weak or empty (crime_00003, 00015, 00022). Having test files is not the same as having coverage.\n\n## Order of work before rollout\n\n1. **Unify `Basket`** and make the subtotal test assert real values. This is medium effort and the riskiest item.\n2. **Propagate the `createSession` error**, and add a failure-path test.\n3. **Check where `endpoint()` gets its host.** `src/core/config.ts:3` hardcodes `localhost:8081` (crime_00009) and has one importer. If that importer is the path that leads to `endpoint()`, checkout will post to localhost in production, and step 2 is the only thing that would make that visible. This is a quick check, and it's a blocker if the answer is yes. Look at the `/Users/buildbot/...` path on line 5 (crime_00010) while you're there.\n4. **Check that checkout's plan tiers match pricing's.** `session.ts:58/60/89` compare against `\"enterprise\"` and `\"pro\"` on their own, separately from `core/pricing.ts:18-19` (crime_00006/00020). Read them side by side, and only fix them if they already disagree. Merging them into one helper can wait.\n5. **Read the 3 TODOs in `session.ts:10-12`** (crime_00029). They're in the file you're shipping, so confirm none of them marks a missing piece of the flow.\n6. **Fix the other two weak session tests**, and add `vitest` to `package.json` (crime_00025) so a clean CI install can run the tests.\n\n## Leave until after the rollout (but file tickets)\n\nThese are real problems, some of them high severity. They're deferred only because they're outside checkout, not because they're harmless:\n\n- **`src/legacy/reconcile.ts:28-31` mixes UTC and local date methods** (crime_00004, high). Results shift by the server's timezone offset.\n- **The admin/plan rule is copied in `pricing.ts:6` and `settlement.ts:49`** (crime_00005, high). Move it into one shared function after the rollout, unless checkout calls `canPrice`. If it does, move it up to item 4.\n- **Payouts and settlement retry POSTs with no idempotency key and swallow errors** (crime_00007, 00008, 00011, 00012). These are a double-payment risk and need to be first in line after the rollout.\n- **Cleanup:** direct `Date.now()` calls (00013, 00021, 00023, 00026), commented-out code (00014, 00016), TODO density in legacy files (00027, 00028), and the `\"POST\"` string repeated across files (00024).\n\nThe scan ranks recent code higher, which is why checkout floats to the top. That's the right focus for this week. Just don't let the legacy items above slip once the rollout is done.",
